MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The 'imageData' slot of an MSImageSet object can be indexed as a datacube.

For example,

library(CardinalWorkflows)
data(pig206)
img1 <- imageData(pig206)[1,,] # get 2-d matrix of first ion
img2 <- imageData(pig206)[features(pig206, mz=888),,] # get 2-d matrix of m/z 888

These will give you the actual intensity matrix of the ion image, which you can then use or plot however you want.

To convert the intensities to colors, you could do, for example,

img3 <- as.raster(img2, max=max(img2, na.rm=TRUE))

to convert it to a raster object.

If you simply want to plot without axes/labels, without manipulating the intensity values, then use R's graphic parameters (see ?par).

For example, to plot an ion image without any axes or labels, you can do

par(bty='n', xaxt='n', yaxt='n')
image(pig206, mz=888, strip=FALSE, colorkey=FALSE, xlab="", ylab="")

Hope this helps!

